  "worked_solution": "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(\\theta )}{{x}^{\u2032}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta =\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi +\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\pi \\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\pi ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi -\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot (-1)-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\pi \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi } & =\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(\\pi )}{{x}^{\u2032}(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\pi }{(-\\frac{1}{\\pi })} \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{ln}\u2061\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
